1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,792
The brush uncovers gold — a thick, heavy coin, well struck.

2
00:00:04,792 --> 00:00:11,448
Gold coinage on this scale is new in India, and it is not a sign of conquest so much as of trade.

3
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:19,689
Somebody was earning a great deal of foreign bullion, and this episode is about how.

4
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:25,808
Coins again, in quantity and in gold.

5
00:00:25,808 --> 00:00:30,922
Inscriptions, including some that give regnal years and let us build a chronology.

6
00:00:30,922 --> 00:00:34,988
Chinese accounts, because the Kushanas sat on the route to China.

7
00:00:34,988 --> 00:00:38,800
And Buddhist texts, which remember Kanishka as a patron.

8
00:00:42,133 --> 00:00:47,171
The Kushanas came out of Central Asia, from a people the Chinese sources call the Yuezhi.

9
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:10,744
Under Kujula Kadphises and his successors they built a state that stretched from Central Asia across the north-west and into the Ganga plains,

10
00:01:10,744 --> 00:01:14,405
with capitals at Purushapura — modern Peshawar — and Mathura.

11
00:01:14,405 --> 00:01:18,130
That shape matters: they held both ends of a trade route.

12
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:21,037
Which route?

13
00:01:21,037 --> 00:01:25,119
The Silk Road, and the Kushanas sat astride its southern stretch.

14
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:31,048
Silk moving west from China, and Indian goods joining it; Roman gold coming the other way.

15
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:38,634
Historians have long argued that the Kushanas melted down Roman gold to strike their own coins,

16
00:01:38,634 --> 00:01:41,707
which is one explanation for the sudden abundance of gold.

17
00:01:41,707 --> 00:01:45,601
Either way, the wealth is real and it is visible in what they built.

18
00:01:50,333 --> 00:01:52,484
Kanishka the First is the ruler to know.

19
00:01:52,484 --> 00:01:57,416
He is remembered as a patron of Buddhism who convened the fourth Buddhist council in Kashmir,

20
00:01:57,416 --> 00:02:02,333
where the texts were compiled and commented on, and where Mahayana ideas gained ground.

21
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:15,026
He also sent Buddhism further along the routes his empire controlled, which is how it reaches Central Asia and China.

22
00:02:15,026 --> 00:02:18,373
His coins carry deities from several traditions — Iranian,

23
00:02:18,373 --> 00:02:23,607
Greek and Indian — which historians read as a deliberately inclusive religious policy.

24
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:36,534
Under the Kushanas the two sculptural schools flourish: Gandhara, with its Hellenistic drapery and grey schist,

25
00:02:36,534 --> 00:02:40,784
and Mathura, working in red sandstone with a distinctly Indian idiom.

26
00:02:40,784 --> 00:02:44,910
Again, we are looking at drapery and ornament, not at figures.

27
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:52,370
And the era question: an era beginning in 78 CE is widely associated with Kanishka,

28
00:02:52,370 --> 00:02:59,341
and that era is the Shaka era used in the Indian national calendar — though the association is disputed.

29
00:03:07,033 --> 00:03:10,874
This is one of the longest-running arguments in Indian chronology.

30
00:03:10,874 --> 00:03:14,958
The traditional view ties Kanishka to the era beginning in 78 CE.

31
00:03:18,667 --> 00:03:27,519
A substantial body of recent scholarship argues for about 127 CE instead, on the evidence of inscriptions and Chinese records.

32
00:03:27,519 --> 00:03:34,093
Indian textbooks generally give 78 CE, so learn that, and know that the question is live.

33
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:39,445
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:39,445 --> 00:03:40,236
Kanishka.

35
00:03:40,236 --> 00:03:41,819
The fourth council.

36
00:03:41,819 --> 00:03:43,306
The Silk Road.

37
00:03:47,733 --> 00:03:48,529
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:48,529 --> 00:03:50,039
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:50,039 --> 00:03:55,049
First: the Kushana capital in the north-west, modern Peshawar.

40
00:03:55,049 --> 00:03:56,383
Purushapura.

41
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:03,597
Second: the Buddhist council Kanishka convened, held in Kashmir.

42
00:04:03,597 --> 00:04:04,372
The fourth.

43
00:04:04,372 --> 00:04:11,451
Third: the trade route whose southern stretch the Kushanas controlled, carrying silk west and gold east.

44
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:17,288
The Silk Road.

45
00:04:17,288 --> 00:04:23,185
In Mains, a recurring question asks why Buddhism spread into Central Asia and China.

46
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:33,351
Use the Kushanas: an empire astride the routes, a patron ruler, monasteries on the roads, and merchants who funded them.

47
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:41,964
Kushana gold takes its place in the gallery, struck from metal that may have travelled half the known world.

48
00:04:45,633 --> 00:04:52,185
Next gallery: south of everything we have looked at so far, three kingdoms and the poetry that records them.

49
00:04:52,185 --> 00:04:55,912
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

